1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,920
NASA says it is building
a base on the Moon.

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:06,180
None of the Moon Base
missions has landed yet.

3
00:00:08,030 --> 00:00:10,930
The first three have slipped to 2027.

4
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:12,850
And to chase this base,

5
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:17,410
the agency put its own planned space
station around the Moon on hold.

6
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:19,870
So why did NASA pause Gateway?

7
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:23,010
And what is real here,
and what is still on paper?

8
00:00:24,390 --> 00:00:30,370
In March 2026, NASA Administrator
Jared Isaacman paused Gateway,

9
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:33,330
a planned outpost in lunar orbit.

10
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:35,570
The pause is indefinite.

11
00:00:35,830 --> 00:00:39,170
The stated goal was to focus
on a base on the surface.

12
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:42,190
Notice the word paused.

13
00:00:42,690 --> 00:00:47,210
NASA says Gateway is not cancelled,
and its hardware may be repurposed.

14
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:50,190
But much of that hardware
was already built,

15
00:00:50,370 --> 00:00:52,270
and it is not easy to reuse.

16
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:56,310
The effort moves from orbit
down to the ground,

17
00:00:56,550 --> 00:00:57,770
near the polar ice.

18
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:04,500
The fate of partners like Europe,
Japan and Canada is still unresolved.

19
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:09,640
The price tag is about twenty billion
dollars over the first seven years,

20
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:14,120
and about thirty billion over
a decade for all three phases.

21
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:16,160
Now, the word base.

22
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:18,480
Picture a city under a dome.

23
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:21,820
That is not what NASA
describes, at least not yet.

24
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,240
There are three phases.

25
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:30,180
Phase One, now to 2029, is robots,
tech demos and scouting.

26
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:36,260
Phase Two, 2029 to 2032, adds early power,

27
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:37,960
cargo and communications.

28
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:44,340
Only Phase Three, from 2032,
is the permanent outpost with habitats.

29
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:48,560
So today, the Moon Base
is a schedule of robotic missions.

30
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,320
Not a building. But why the south pole?

31
00:01:52,580 --> 00:01:54,400
Of all the places on the Moon?

32
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:56,380
The first reason is water.

33
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:00,780
Parts of the polar craters
never see sunlight.

34
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:04,720
They can reach about minus two
hundred fifty degrees Celsius.

35
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:08,060
That is colder than the surface of Pluto.

36
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:12,860
In that cold, water ice can
survive for a very long time.

37
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:18,320
In 2009, NASA's LCROSS
slammed into Cabeus crater,

38
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:22,400
near the south pole,
and the plume confirmed water ice.

39
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,300
It held about one hundred
fifty five kilograms of water.

40
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:29,040
That is only what the plume let us see.

41
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:30,580
It is not a total.

42
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:35,560
Chandrayaan-3 measured
temperatures at high latitudes,

43
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:38,360
but it did not directly detect ice.

44
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:41,600
So the evidence that ice exists is solid.

45
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:45,460
What is thin is how much
there is, what form it takes,

46
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:47,600
and whether anyone can mine it.

47
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:52,840
And this is ice mixed
into soil, not lakes.

48
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:55,200
And here is the loop I cannot close.

49
00:02:55,720 --> 00:02:56,720
Where did it come from?

50
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:02,280
Comets, asteroids,
the solar wind, even Earth.

51
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:04,740
Scientists have not settled that.

52
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:06,540
Hold on to that question.

53
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:08,820
There is a second reason for the pole.

54
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:12,380
Light. The idea
of a sunless pole is wrong.

55
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:16,740
Some ridges there get
near continuous sunlight,

56
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:19,120
while the shadowed craters sit nearby.

57
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:22,120
Sunlight for power, ice in the dark.

58
00:03:22,180 --> 00:03:24,160
Close together. That is the pitch.

59
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:26,320
Now, who is actually going?

60
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:28,060
NASA numbers the missions.

61
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:34,120
Moon Base One is a Blue Origin lander
aimed at the Shackleton Connecting Ridge.

62
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:37,440
Moon Base Two is an Astrobotic lander,

63
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:41,020
and Moon Base Three
is an Intuitive Machines lander.

64
00:03:41,060 --> 00:03:45,500
NASA is buying rides,
not building every machine itself.

65
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:47,960
Those rides have slipped.

66
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:51,160
On May twenty eighth, 2026,

67
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:56,260
New Glenn exploded during a static fire
test while being prepared for launch.

68
00:03:56,660 --> 00:04:01,120
That explosion pushed Moon Base
One, the Blue Origin lander,

69
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:03,880
to the first part of 2027.

70
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:09,860
Moon Base III also slipped,
to the first quarter of 2027,

71
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:11,520
according to its maker.

72
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:13,620
What comes after the landers?

73
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:15,200
Two strange machines.

74
00:04:16,620 --> 00:04:21,300
First, PROMISE. A six wheeled
rover powered by a nuclear source.

75
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:25,360
It carries a drill that reaches
about one meter down,

76
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:28,700
and it is designed
for an eighteen month mission.

77
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:34,620
Blue Origin won a ninety four million
dollar contract to deliver it,

78
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:37,180
with a one hundred forty
million dollar option

79
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:39,820
and a target of late 2028.

80
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:45,240
Second, MoonFall. Four
hopping drones from JPL,

81
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:48,820
planned for 2028. Not wheels.

82
00:04:49,340 --> 00:04:52,420
Hops. Their job is to map landing sites,

83
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:56,180
flying across a lunar day
of about fourteen Earth days.

84
00:04:56,630 --> 00:04:59,420
Later comes the part
that feels like a base.

85
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:03,100
A pressurized rover
from Japan's space agency.

86
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:06,660
A mobile lab where crews
work without spacesuits,

87
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:09,240
on missions of up to twenty eight days.

88
00:05:09,580 --> 00:05:11,200
Which brings us to power.

89
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:15,880
A lunar day is about fourteen
Earth days of sunlight,

90
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:17,660
then fourteen of night.

91
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:22,620
The polar ridges are an exception,
with near continuous sun.

92
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:25,880
But the crater floors never see light.

93
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:28,920
Power through darkness
is the hard problem.

94
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:33,240
NASA is aiming for a fission
reactor of about one hundred

95
00:05:33,280 --> 00:05:36,980
kilowatts on the surface by around 2030.

96
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:40,620
Experts doubt the timeline
more than the size.

97
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:45,560
A working surface reactor
by 2030 is a steep climb.

98
00:05:46,060 --> 00:05:49,200
And that is the honest tension
at the center of this story.

99
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:50,940
Can the schedule hold?

100
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:59,160
NASA is targeting Artemis Three
as a 2027 lander test in Earth orbit,

101
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:02,440
and Artemis Four
as the first crewed landing,

102
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,860
in early 2028.

103
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:10,220
Experts question the 2027 and 2028 dates.

104
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:13,940
The Artemis crewed lander
still needs development.

105
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:17,880
The pole has steep slopes,
ridges and deep craters.

106
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:20,300
Add dust and extreme cold.

107
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:23,320
Even a perfect landing is not yet mining.

108
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:25,580
So the rebel question is this.

109
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:29,180
Is the Moon Base a plan,
or a promise on a slide?

110
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:34,580
Now the race. China is also
aiming for the south pole.

111
00:06:35,720 --> 00:06:40,180
Its Chang'e-7 mission, built to look
for water ice near Shackleton,

112
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:44,000
was postponed in August to 2027.

113
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:48,300
China plans a crewed
landing by around 2030.

114
00:06:48,940 --> 00:06:52,460
It is also planning a lunar
research station with Russia,

115
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:55,520
with construction from about 2031.

116
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:58,680
Those dates are not firm, on either side.

117
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:00,860
So this is not a clean sprint.

118
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:05,160
Two programs, both
with slipping calendars,

119
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:07,340
both eyeing the Shackleton area.

120
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:11,100
That is a reason for speed,
and a reason for caution.

121
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:17,120
NASA is even inviting students in, with
a one million dollar Moon Base Challenge.

122
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:19,520
A base is a long project.

123
00:07:20,060 --> 00:07:26,100
Phase Three, in 2032 and beyond, belongs
to people who are in school right now.

124
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:28,200
So let's go back to the opening.

125
00:07:28,580 --> 00:07:30,840
Why did NASA pause its own station?

126
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:34,320
Because the base is not a place.

127
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:39,480
It is a sequence.
Landers first, then rovers,

128
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:41,320
then power, then habitats.

129
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:44,220
Each step depends on the one before it.

130
00:07:44,540 --> 00:07:47,660
NASA chose to spend its effort
on that surface sequence,

131
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:51,300
near the ice, and Gateway
is paused, not dead.

132
00:07:52,260 --> 00:07:56,060
So the better question
is not why NASA paused a station.

133
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:58,960
It is whether the surface
sequence can hold,

134
00:07:59,140 --> 00:08:04,860
step by step. Whether the first
landers reach the surface in 2027.

135
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:08,420
Whether the reactor stays near 2030.

136
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:12,760
And whether anyone can show
that the ice is usable,

137
00:08:13,080 --> 00:08:14,300
and where it came from.

138
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:17,840
The base is still mostly on paper.

139
00:08:18,180 --> 00:08:21,280
The next two years decide
whether it becomes hardware.

140
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:23,660
And either way,

141
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:28,020
the south pole is where both NASA
and China are placing their bets.

142
00:08:28,470 --> 00:08:34,220
If you want to follow it with us,
subscribe to Rebel Minds Cosmos.

143
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:38,540
Next time, we go inside
those dark craters,

144
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:41,240
and ask where that ice came from.
